  "payload": {
    "role": "assistant",
    "type": "assistant_message",
    "content": "All 26 checklist items have been tested and recorded.\n\n**Result: 21 PASS / 5 FAIL**\n\nThe core product works well — browsing, template selection, form filling, Markdown and plain-text preview rendering, favorites, filtering/sorting, suggestions, copy-to-clipboard, custom template submission, and localStorage persistence all behave correctly.\n\nThe 5 failures cluster around **validation gaps**:\n\n- **FT-10** — Search has no fuzzy matching. `\"resme\"` and `\"meetng\"` each return \"Showing 0 of 6\" with no suggestions; matching is strict substring only.\n- **CS-14** — The `type=email` field accepts `\"not-an-email\"` with no error and still enables the preview, rendering the invalid value into the output.\n- **CS-17** — Special-character filtering is incomplete. It blocks `< > { } [ ] \\ | \\``, but permits `#`, `*`, `-`, so `\"## Alice **Bold** ---\"` in Full Name rendered as `<strong>Bold</strong>` inside the generated H1.\n- **CS-27** — No placeholder/field consistency check. A template with content placeholders `{{owner}}`/`{{done}}` but fields `Owner`/`Unused Extra` submitted cleanly, and its preview showed the literal unresolved `{{done}}`.\n- **CS-29** — Duplicate field labels are silently accepted; adding \"Owner\" twice produced two entries sharing the identical key, so they collapse onto one placeholder instead of being independently fillable.\n\nWorth noting: CS-27 and CS-29 compound each other — both let a structurally broken template reach the library, where the damage only surfaces later at preview time. Also, CS-19 passed on its blank/whitespace enforcement (which is solid, including trimming), but its \"valid value\" clause is undermined by the same email gap recorded under CS-14, so I scoped that verdict to the nonblank behavior rather than double-counting the defect."
  }
}
